t={q-k\over D}\le1.82516.
\]

No new geometric input is required.

## 1. Audited scalar endpoint

The merged `g=0.0050` theorem uses

\[
C_0=2\sqrt{\Delta_2}+0.0050,
\qquad
\Delta_2={\pi\over2\sqrt3},
\]

and the one-variable contradiction function

\[
H_{50}(t)=\Phi(K_{50}(t))-{R_{50}(t)\over2},
\]

where

\[
R_{50}(t)={9t\over2}-2\pi-C_0,
\]

\[
K_{50}(t)=\pi+{R_{50}(t)\over2}-t-2C_0+{8\Delta_2\over C_0},
\]

and

\[
\Phi(z)=z-6\arcsin(\tanh(z/6)).
\]

The hostile audit checks

\[
H_{50}(1.82515)=2.5373341\times10^{-5}>0,
\]

\[
\boxed{H_{50}(1.82516)=3.1640100\times10^{-6}>0,}
\]

\[
H_{50}(1.82517)=-1.9045315\times10^{-5}<0,
\]

and brackets the unique scalar zero by

\[
1.82516142463113334<t_*<1.82516142463113335.
\]

## 2. Monotonicity makes the promotion rigorous

The same audit proves

\[
K_{50}'(t)=5/4
\]

and, on the relevant orbit-mass interval,

\[
\Phi'(K)<0.025.
\]

Hence

\[
H_{50}'(t)={5\over4}\Phi'(K_{50}(t))-{9\over4}<0.
\]

Thus \(H_{50}\) is strictly decreasing. Since \(H_{50}(1.82516)>0\), every
\(t\le1.82516\) has positive contradiction margin and is excluded.

### Theorem — promoted joint-orbit floor

Every convergent full-core Type-II survivor subsequence in the merged proof-only scope satisfies

\[
\boxed{
\liminf_{D\to\infty}{q-k\over D}\ge1.82516.
}
\]

This is a safe round promotion below the hostile-audited root \(t_*\). It does not alter canonical theorem state and does not close C-II.

## 3. Frontier

Further decimal tuning is low value. The joint route relaxation is already exact for its two local costs. The next useful work is structural: dominant-orbit terminal routing, outer-to-outer train bracing, exceptional-face closure rank, or a simultaneous source-preserving train shear.
